sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-13> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The building of agglutinated shells from sand-grains, spicules or diatom frustules by rhizopods such as Difflugia is a purely physical consequence of foreign particles being trapped in the surface film by surface energy, with selectivity explained by specific surface-energies rather than volition.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Agglutinated protist shells form by surface energy, not choice or skill";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IX, “On Concretions, Spicules, and Spicular Skeletons”; topic “Agglutinated shells of rhizopods”. Scope: agglutinated testate amoebae and foraminifera"
}
